CA NEXUS
Extreme Detail UI/UX, Screen, Interaction & Frontend Implementation Specification
Version 1.0 | September 2026
Purpose: This document converts the CA Nexus Complete Product, Functional, Workflow & Business Blueprint into an implementation-ready product specification for a UI designer and an AI frontend coding agent. It defines application structure, navigation, every major screen, layouts, components, interactions, states, object relationships, workflows and implementation rules.
Visual/Layout Reference: Use the supplied Next Shadcn Admin Dashboard repository as the base application shell and design system. Preserve its dashboard layout philosophy, sidebar/header behavior, card density, table patterns, dialogs, sheets, charts, filters, responsiveness, theme support and Shadcn component conventions. CA Nexus must be implemented by extending the existing repository rather than replacing its design architecture.
Source Product Basis: CA Nexus Complete Product, Functional, Workflow & Business Blueprint v3.0.
1. Product Intent and UX Principle
CA Nexus is a connected operating system for the post-client lifecycle of a Chartered Accountant or CA firm. The interface must make connected work visible. Users should not experience Clients, Matters, Compliance, Tasks, Documents, Communications and Billing as isolated applications.
Primary operational journey:
Client → Service/Matter → Compliance/Workflow → Tasks → Document Collection → Communication → Review → Completion/Filing → Time & Billing → Reporting
Every major record must show its connected context and linked records.
Every dashboard number must drill into a useful filtered list.
Every list must support operational action, not only viewing.
Every detail page must answer: What is this? What is its current status? What is pending? Who owns it? What is due next? What can I do now?
Use dense, professional enterprise layouts suitable for high-volume office operations.
Use badges, progress indicators, timelines and status labels consistently.
Do not rely on color alone to communicate risk, status or priority.
Internal collaboration and client communication must be visually distinct.
Permission-restricted actions may be hidden or disabled depending on the implementation model, but the frontend must support permission-aware rendering.
